00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.000
We also announced a feature
called max instances.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.950
And these are basically
scaling controls

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.180
and the idea is when you
send traffic to a function,

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.730
we automatically spin
up new instances for you

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.650
to handle that traffic.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.600
Sometimes that's great.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.120
Other times, you might
want to control the degree

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.830
to which your function scales.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:53.700
So a great example here is
you have a SQL database that

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.270
can only tolerate up to 10
connections at the same time.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.450
Well, if you get 1,000 requests
to a function and each of those

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.210
hits your database, you
might break some things.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.550
So max instances
allows you to say, hey,

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.302
only spawn 10 instances.

00:04:07.302 --> 00:04:08.760
And that will allow
me to make sure

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.890
that my serverless
stuff works very

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.400
well with my non-scaling
non-serverless stuff.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.040
The last feature
that is important

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.500
here is serverless VPC access.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.079
And the idea here is Cloud
Functions traditionally

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:26.220
has not been able to communicate
with stuff inside of your VPC

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:27.360
network.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.980
We now support
this feature, which

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.550
allows you to communicate with
your Google Compute Engine

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:33.420
instances.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.693
Cloud Memorystore for
Redis is a huge one.

00:04:35.693 --> 00:04:37.110
I hear from customers
all the time

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.260
that they'd love to be able to
support talking to their Cloud

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.310
Memorystore instances.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.460
You can now access those
from Cloud Functions.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.910
You can also access resources
in paired VPC networks

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:53.710
and on-premises networks that
you access through cloud VPN.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:57.060
OK, enough recap about
the new stuff we launched.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.810
Cloud Functions 101,
I'm going to walk you

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.720
through the developer experience
so that people can see what

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.760
is this thing actually about.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:08.660
This is an example of a
function written in Node.js.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.090
You export a function
called hello, World.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.190
This specifically
is an HTTP function,

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.670
which means that we want
to make the experience very

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.680
seamless and very familiar.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:22.430
So you get HTTP objects
that are familiar to you

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:24.450
as a Node developer.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:27.610
Any Node developers
in the audience?

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:28.160
Yeah, a few.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.150
OK, cool.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:32.060
So this is based on Express.js.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.820
Express is a popular web
application framework,

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:38.455
so request and response
are objects that are--

00:05:38.455 --> 00:05:40.310
they're those objects
from that framework.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:41.810
So this should all
be very familiar.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:43.410
So you write this function.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:46.130
It takes these arguments, and
then it sends a message back

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:46.910
to the caller--

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:48.290
Hello, Cloud Next.

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:51.613
Very simple, three
lines of code.

00:05:51.613 --> 00:05:54.030
The next thing you do is you
want to deploy that function.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:56.240
So we provide a
tool called G Cloud.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.410
G Cloud is the tool that you
use to manage all of your Google

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.300
Cloud Platform resources.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.160
You say G Cloud
Functions deploy.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.410
My function name
here is helloWorld.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:09.890
You specify that you want it
to use the runtime nodejs8.

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:11.460
And you specify a trigger.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.170
This is the HTTP trigger.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:17.100
It's going to give you
a URL, through which you

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.360
can access your function.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.550
That looks something
like region project dot

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.340
cloudfunctions.net/helloWorld.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:24.990
You can now visit
this in your browser.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:28.573
You can curl it, and it will
respond back with that message.

00:06:28.573 --> 00:06:30.990
And so what we showed you here
is it's super simple to get

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:31.500
started.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.210
You write three lines of code.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:34.710
You issue one command.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.240
And from that,
you get this thing

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.310
that just kind of magically
works in the cloud.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.610
It scales for you.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.990
It does the sort of resource
based billing model where

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:44.580
you only pay for what you use.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.950
And all you had to think
about was this tiny little bit

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.680
of code that you wrote.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.660
So these are really
simple building blocks.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.078
And you can build really
powerful things with them.

00:06:54.078 --> 00:06:55.620
We see a couple of
different patterns

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.300
that customers often use.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.120
I hear people ask me how should
I use this product, how should

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:00.863
I think about it.

00:07:00.863 --> 00:07:03.030
So Justin, you want to talk
about some of the things

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:04.000
you see among customers?

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:05.417
JUSTIN GRAYSTON:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:05.417 --> 00:07:08.990
I mean, Cloud Functions are
really powerful and flexible.

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:11.130
But I think these
three areas are

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.620
where you see a lot of
customers using them,

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:14.820
event-based architectures.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:17.130
So a company based
out of London,

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.920
a media company
called Seven Digital,

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.293
used Google Cloud
Storage triggers,

00:07:23.293 --> 00:07:24.960
and then using that
with Cloud Functions

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.780
to create a complete new
content supply chain.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.200
And you have another
pattern that I've

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.620
seen quite a lot is where
you're decoupling on-prem legacy

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:39.240
systems, which have been
tied to consumer-facing apps.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.210
And now those systems, you
need to keep them around.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:43.408
But they're at web scale.

00:07:43.408 --> 00:07:44.450
STEWART REICHLING: Right.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:47.670
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: So by using
Pub/Sub and Cloud Functions,

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.465
you can then decouple
those systems.

00:07:49.465 --> 00:07:51.090
STEWART REICHLING:
OK, so the idea here

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.940
is you write a
function, you deploy it,

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.250
you say, hey, I want this
function to run when somebody

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.500
maybe puts a file in
Google Cloud Storage

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.650
or when someone puts a
message on a Pub/Sub topic.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:03.590
JUSTIN GRAYSTON:
Yeah, so let's say

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:09.550
somebody buys something, that
goes into a legacy database.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.247
You then put that
transaction into Pub/Sub.

00:08:12.247 --> 00:08:13.830
And then you can
have a Cloud Function

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:15.913
that runs and then puts
that into, say, Firestore,

00:08:15.913 --> 00:08:17.415
which is a web scale database.

00:08:17.415 --> 00:08:18.790
STEWART REICHLING:
Cool, awesome.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:22.060
The other category we see
often is data processing.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:25.950
So I talked to a company
out of Europe, In Farm.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:28.710
They do really cool stuff
in the agriculture space.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.800
They have farms distributed
throughout Europe.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.669
And at each of these farms, they
have IoT sensors, small devices

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.460
that transmit messages
back saying what

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.980
the status is of those sensors.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:44.760
And they use Cloud Functions
via a Pub/Sub topic

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.310
that carries that
sensor data to them.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:48.600
They aggregate that data.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.220
They put it in
different databases.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:52.710
They put it in a
dashboard so that they

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:55.650
can see the status
of their farms

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.460
across different countries.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.640
And so what you see here
is a very simple sort

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:03.750
of data processing model
where you get messages.

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:05.940
You're doing white ETL.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.950
And then you put
those somewhere else.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.550
Cloud Function is a great way
to glue that stuff together.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.050
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Yeah, and Cloud
Automation is another thing

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:16.530
that we've seen, is where you
have multiple cloud products,

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.870
and you may want to use
the Cloud Functions to glue

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.450
those products together.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.790
So let's say, for
example, everything

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.980
is going into the
Stackdriver logs.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.500
Somebody creates a
VM or a firewall.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.440
You can create a filter and
create a sink in Stackdriver,

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:32.850
which goes to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:35.880
And you can tie your Cloud
Function to that Pub/Sub topic.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.510
And that Cloud Function can
then check with Cloud Security

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:44.520
Command Center whether that's a
policy violation, for example,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.030
whether a firewall has been
created that there is to open.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:51.200
And then the Cloud Function
can then report that violation.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:52.200
STEWART REICHLING: Cool.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.340
Yeah, so Stackdriver, for
people who don't know,

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:57.103
is the suite of logging
and monitoring tools.

00:09:57.103 --> 00:09:58.770
Pretty much all of
Google Cloud products

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.260
are very well
integrated with it.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:05.610
The idea, again, is from a Cloud
Function or when a VM spins up,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:08.580
I can go console
log some message.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.680
Imagine like console log error.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.480
I can then say, hey,
I'm going to filter

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.430
messages that contain error.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.590
Based on those, do something.

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.320
Send maybe an email to
your security person

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:22.140
or to whoever is in charge
of reliability, send an SMS.

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.780
It's a very flexible way for
you to kind of glue together

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:27.481
your cloud products and
what you're doing in GCP.

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:33.440
But it all starts with
one simple, gcloud

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.510
deploy myFunction--

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.310
gcloud functions
deploy myFunction.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:40.340
And this command looks
deceptively simple,

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.440
but it comes with a whole
bunch of cool stuff.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.160
So for the price of one gcloud
command, what are you getting?

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.890
Clearly when you write
three lines of code and type

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:52.610
this one command, that's not
really enough for your function

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:54.080
to then be running in our cloud.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.300
So what's happening
behind the scenes?

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:57.980
One is you have a build step.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:01.250
During that build step, we
take your code, we upload it.

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:04.520
We then start a Cloud Build job.

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:06.770
Cloud Build id our
tool, our product that

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:08.930
allows you to build containers.

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:10.970
We install dependencies.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:12.470
If you're a node
developer, it would

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:15.560
use NPM Install, which
is super familiar to you

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:18.080
if it's standard
Node.js tooling.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.220
We add some code that
runs your function.

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:22.800
We're going to talk about
that more in a second.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.380
And we build a container.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:26.180
And our infrastructure
cloud functions

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:27.920
are really just
containers running

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:30.210
on our serverless
container infrastructure.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:34.040
So the output of this step is
that you have a container image

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:36.380
that contains your code,
some additional code,

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:38.060
and your dependencies.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.230
What happens next?

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:41.420
We deploy your function.

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:43.850
Your function-- we
take that container.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:45.510
We're going to connect
a trigger to it.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.700
So if you say HTTP, we're
going to give you a URL.

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:50.990
We're going to create an
instance of your function

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:52.998
that runs in our data center.

00:11:52.998 --> 00:11:54.290
We then perform a health check.

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:56.248
We make sure that what
you gave us can actually

00:11:56.248 --> 00:11:58.200
run on our infrastructure.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.800
And then we start to migrate
traffic over gradually.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:02.930
And so now you
have this container

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:05.780
that is ready to receive
requests on our infrastructure.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:07.380
You wrote a couple
lines of code.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:08.750
We built a container image.

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:11.160
We made sure it actually runs,
and now it's ready to run.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.520
So the last thing
we do is we run it.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:16.280
When you send traffic to
it, we route those requests

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:17.420
to your function.

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:19.950
We auto scale instances
of your function.

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:21.740
So if you're not
receiving any requests,

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:24.920
you have zero instances,
you're not paying for anything,

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:26.308
it scales down to zero.

00:12:26.308 --> 00:12:27.850
You get a ton of
request, we're going

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:29.750
to scale up a whole
bunch of instances

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:32.930
to meet whatever
your QPS needs are.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.390
We invoke your function
when a request comes in,

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.768
and we generate some
logs and metrics.

00:12:37.768 --> 00:12:39.810
One of the challenges that
we hear from customers

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.270
is around
observability, and so we

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:44.030
want to make sure that when
you're using Cloud Functions,

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:47.270
you have a very easy way to
see what's actually going on.

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:49.160
What are the errors
that are generated?

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.190
What are the log statements
that you're outputting?

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:55.220
And so again, you went
from three lines of code,

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:58.430
one gcloud command to an
auto scaling function that

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:01.100
responds to inbound requests.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:03.360
We handle a lot of the
gory details for you

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.970
so that this is
actually very simple.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:08.430
And this is great
for most scenarios.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.130
When you create a managed
product like Cloud Functions,

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.680
you want to try and
solve as many use cases

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.480
as you possibly can.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:17.640
But often when you're
working on a project,

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:21.210
you might have one
specific need that kind of

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:25.110
falls outside of
the realm of what is

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:27.270
supported on Cloud Functions.

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:30.060
And it doesn't happen too
often, but when it does happen,

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:31.380
it's really annoying.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.250
And your resolution
there often is--

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:36.270
I try to talk to
customers all the time.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.230
The customer will come
to me and say, hey,

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:41.670
could you please add this
custom binary to my container?

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:44.260
Because I need to have
that run inside of it.

00:13:44.260 --> 00:13:46.620
Well, the way Cloud
Functions is built today,

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.530
what actually would happen if I
put that custom binary in there

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:52.650
is that everyone's function
suddenly has that thing living

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:53.760
inside of their container.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.290
Clearly that's not tenable.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.780
If it's a hugely popular
binary, then yeah,

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.090
we'll add that to support
a lot of use cases.

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.640
But one single customer's
really specific

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:05.700
thing is hard to add because you
have to convince everybody else

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:07.570
that they actually want that.

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:10.090
So what if you actually
need more control?

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:13.120
What if you need more control
around building your function?

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.487
The example of a custom
binary is a great one.

00:14:15.487 --> 00:14:17.320
You want to have something
run inside of it.

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:21.710
What do you do if the product
does not support that?

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:26.480
What do you do if you need
more control over deployments?

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:28.400
So as I mentioned,
Cloud Functions

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:30.830
is live in six
different regions.

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:32.640
What if you need
your function to run

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:35.390
in a very specific region for--

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:37.950
I don't know--
regulatory reasons?

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:41.000
What do you do if
your function needs

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.750
specific resources to run?

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:45.080
So a great example is
today on Cloud Functions,

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:49.730
we offer five different
instance types ranging in memory

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:53.090
from 128 megs all
the way up to 2,048.

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:54.410
Well, what if you need more?

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:55.970
What if you need more CPU?

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.680
What if you need GPUs?

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:00.260
Again, you could
send me an email

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:03.050
and say, hey, I'd really
love to support GPUs,

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:06.150
and I'll tell you, yes,
we'd love to do that too.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:09.210
It might be six months
or a year from now.

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:12.360
Well, you need to solve
that problem today.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:16.840
A great way to explain this
is to go back to the basics.

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:18.550
What is a function?

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:22.500
Well, as I explained earlier,
it's really just a container.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:26.350
And that runs on our serverless
container infrastructure.

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:29.457
That infrastructure
is managed by Google.

00:15:29.457 --> 00:15:31.790
Within that container, there
are a few different things.

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:35.410
One of those is an operating
system, Ubuntu in most cases.

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:37.780
And this is the value
of being on a managed

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:41.330
product, is that we patch
that operating system for you.

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:43.450
We make sure that,
the best we can,

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.580
there are no security
vulnerabilities in it.

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:48.850
All of that stuff gets
handled for you by us

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.860
when you're on a
managed product.

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:53.440
Inside of that container
is also a runtime.

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:56.110
In this example, it would
be the Node.js binary.

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:57.670
If you're writing
a Python function,

00:15:57.670 --> 00:15:59.900
there's Python inside of there.

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:02.478
And again, that's managed
and patched by us.

00:16:02.478 --> 00:16:05.020
On top of that, there's this
bit of code called the Functions

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:06.037
Framework.

00:16:06.037 --> 00:16:07.870
And we're going to talk
about this in depth.

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:11.050
But the main idea is it manages
your function's lifecycle.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:13.070
You gave us a couple
lines of code.

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:15.550
We put in an operating
system, a binary.

00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:19.630
Clearly that's still not enough
to make your function available

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:20.560
and run it.

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:22.798
The Functions Framework
is what does that.

00:16:22.798 --> 00:16:25.090
And then on top of that,
you've got your function code,

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:26.350
your dependencies.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:29.050
This might look like a simple
Node.js function like the one

00:16:29.050 --> 00:16:30.040
we have there.

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:32.052
You might have a
package JSON file,

00:16:32.052 --> 00:16:33.760
which is what you use
as a node developer

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.790
to specify the dependencies
of your function.

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:40.570
And this is the part
that's managed by you.

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:44.680
Let's talk about this function's
framework a little bit more.

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:47.230
And just to be clear, this
stuff is available today.

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:48.960
It's a real thing.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.810
It is now available
open source also.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:53.380
So I'll talk about
that more in a second.

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:56.200
The Node.js 10 runtime
on Cloud Functions

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:58.510
is based on this
Functions Framework.

00:16:58.510 --> 00:17:00.880
If you deploy a function
today to Cloud Functions

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:03.250
and you say runtime
Node.js 10, you're

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:07.640
going to get the runtime that
is based on this function.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:09.760
It's available as
an NPM package.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:12.400
NPM is Node Package Manager.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:16.030
You can install it using
standard Node.js tooling.

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:19.569
And the source code is
all available in GitHub.

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:21.255
It's an open source project.

00:17:21.255 --> 00:17:21.880
You can see it.

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:24.213
We've had people already
contributing issues, questions.

00:17:24.213 --> 00:17:25.720
You can contribute commits.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:27.190
We love full requests.

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:30.370
And the reason I put this
sticker on top about it now

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:32.740
being open source is
the Functions Framework

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:33.783
has always existed.

00:17:33.783 --> 00:17:35.950
We haven't always called
it the Functions Framework,

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:37.810
but there's clearly
always been this code

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:40.900
that takes your function
and makes it runable.

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:43.505
Now we're making that more
of an open source-- we're

00:17:43.505 --> 00:17:45.130
taking that in an
open source direction

00:17:45.130 --> 00:17:47.620
where you have this package
which you can install if you

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:50.162
want to, and it's going to give
you some nice benefits, which

00:17:50.162 --> 00:17:51.250
we'll walk you through.

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:53.382
And the code is also
fully inspectable

00:17:53.382 --> 00:17:55.340
so that you could go up
and say, hey, actually,

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:56.170
this is a great idea.

00:17:56.170 --> 00:17:57.430
I want to make
some slight tweaks.

00:17:57.430 --> 00:17:58.780
I want to create my own thing.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:03.230
Well, here's a great way
to get started with that.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:06.200
So let's talk about exactly what
the Functions Framework does

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:09.280
so that you have a good sense
of, if I use this code, what's

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:10.750
it actually doing for me?

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.390
And I should also
mention that the stuff

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:15.550
I'm about to talk about--

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:17.800
if you take away two things
from this talk, it's, one,

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:19.450
the Functions
Framework is awesome.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.343
The second one is that it's
completely optional if you're

00:18:22.343 --> 00:18:23.260
using Cloud Functions.

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:25.660
So you can go on deploying
functions the way

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:26.380
you always have.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.422
You don't have to actually
think about this stuff

00:18:28.422 --> 00:18:29.527
or know about this stuff.

00:18:29.527 --> 00:18:31.360
But if you do know about
it, it can give you

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:33.530
some nice benefits.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:35.410
So we've got your container.

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:38.020
Within that container, we're
going to focus on these top two

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:38.520
layers.

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:40.995
The first one is
your function code.

00:18:40.995 --> 00:18:42.862
We've already seen
this a few times now,

00:18:42.862 --> 00:18:43.820
pretty straightforward.

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:47.020
It's an HTTP function, Node.js.

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:50.830
The Functions Framework manages
your function's lifecycle.

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:54.610
And what I mean by that is, OK,
the first thing it does is--

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:57.550
spoiler alert-- it's
actually just an HTTP server

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:58.183
under the hood.

00:18:58.183 --> 00:18:59.600
You don't need to
know about that.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:03.250
But that server, it exposes
your hello world function

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:04.990
on /helloWorld.

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:07.630
So if you send it a
request to /helloWorld,

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:09.943
it's going to run your function.

00:19:09.943 --> 00:19:12.110
The second thing that this
function's framework does

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:14.570
is if you have a
client that sends

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:18.830
a request to your function,
it sends an HTTP request,

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:21.560
well, it's going to
take that HTTP request,

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:25.580
transform it into objects that
are specific to your language,

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:27.530
and it's going to invoke
and run your function

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:29.450
by passing it those arguments.

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:32.060
Again, these are
Express.js arguments.

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:34.430
For Python, these would
be Flask arguments.

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:36.990
For a different language, they
would be something different.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:38.390
But it's very idiomatic.

00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:41.300
It should feel extremely
familiar to a Node.js

00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:42.810
developer.

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:46.470
So that function gets invoked,
passes those arguments.

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:51.900
Your function runs, and it
reaches that res.send block.

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:54.660
It's going to send that back
to the Functions Framework.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:58.860
The Functions Framework takes
that, transforms it into HTTP,

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.027
and sends it back to the client.

00:20:01.027 --> 00:20:02.860
So all this stuff is
pretty straightforward.

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:04.620
You could write this
stuff for yourself

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:07.200
with a couple hundred
lines of code.

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:08.940
Nothing too magical going on.

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:10.980
But the really
nice thing here is

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:13.110
I can just write this
function, deploy it, not

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:14.680
think about any of this stuff.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.130
And I'm just thinking
about my function.

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:20.250
And why that's important
is that you really care--

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:22.770
the value you can
provide as a developer

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:25.530
is in the business logic,
in solving the problems

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:26.910
that your business faces.

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:28.710
You don't really
provide too much value

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:32.670
by writing an HTTP server,
and making sure it's exposed,

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:35.610
and handling, marshaling, or
unmarshaling of arguments.

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:37.883
All that stuff is important,
but it's not really

00:20:37.883 --> 00:20:40.050
what you want to focus on
and spend your development

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:42.380
resources.

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:44.210
So that was a lot of talking.

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:45.953
Let's switch to a demo.

00:20:45.953 --> 00:20:47.870
Justin is going to show
you what this actually

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:49.307
looks like in practice.

00:20:49.307 --> 00:20:50.390
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Yeah, OK.

00:20:50.390 --> 00:20:55.840
So this is the biggest monitor
I think I'm ever going to have.

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.410
Is that all viewable?

00:20:57.410 --> 00:20:58.410
Is that clear enough?

00:20:58.410 --> 00:20:58.910
Yeah?

00:20:58.910 --> 00:21:00.140
It's big enough?

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:03.180
So simple function.

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:06.060
You've seen this time and
time again in the deck.

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:08.010
Nothing fancy going on here.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:12.050
We got a package.json,
node dependencies.

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:13.800
So it's as vanilla as
it can possibly get.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:15.217
STEWART REICHLING:
Can I mention--

00:21:15.217 --> 00:21:17.563
again, as a node developer,
none of this stuff

00:21:17.563 --> 00:21:18.730
should be surprising to you.

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:20.390
This is a Node.js function.

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:23.220
It's an HTTP function, exactly
what we just showed you.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:27.800
Package JSON is the file
that you use to specify--

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:30.820
it describes your
module or your function.

00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:32.495
You can put
dependencies in there.

00:21:32.495 --> 00:21:34.120
All of this stuff,
as a node developer,

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:35.993
should feel completely natural.

00:21:35.993 --> 00:21:36.910
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Yeah.

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:38.960
So first thing we're
going to do is we're just

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:40.238
going to deploy this function.

00:21:40.238 --> 00:21:41.530
We're not going to do anything.

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:43.652
We've just got these
three lines of code,

00:21:43.652 --> 00:21:45.110
and we're going to
deploy this out.

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:49.335
And I'm going to specify
the Node.js runtime.

00:21:49.335 --> 00:21:51.210
STEWART REICHLING: You
can see it right here.

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:53.047
It's very nice.

00:21:53.047 --> 00:21:53.880
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: OK.

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:54.880
Talk amongst yourselves.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:58.300
I'm regretting not having
a bash script right now.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:21:58.900
OK.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:03.220
So the runtime, and I'm going
to create and HTTP trigger.

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:05.220
STEWART REICHLING: And
this is using Node.js 10.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:08.610
The slide I showed earlier
with code was using Node.js 8.

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:11.220
And you're probably
looking at this thinking,

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:12.310
what's the big deal?

