00:26:08.810 --> 00:26:10.310
I'll explain it briefly.

00:26:10.310 --> 00:26:13.490
You can also run your functions
in Knative environments.

00:26:13.490 --> 00:26:15.800
Knative environments
are environments

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:20.510
that run containers that
scale automatically and have--

00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:23.600
they're basically server
environments from a developer

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:25.580
experience perspective.

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:29.870
We announced two products
that are Knative environments

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:31.530
at Cloud Next this year.

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:32.930
One of them is Cloud Run.

00:26:32.930 --> 00:26:35.278
The other one is
Cloud Run on GKE.

00:26:35.278 --> 00:26:36.820
If you don't know
what these are yet,

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:39.060
I'm about to talk about them.

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:42.800
So your Knative environments
basically tell--

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:45.380
it's a standard for
how you run containers

00:26:45.380 --> 00:26:46.710
in a serverless manner.

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:47.900
Your functions scale up.

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:49.150
They scale down.

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:52.880
There's a serving API to which
these two products adhere.

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:56.480
And we can now take your
function with the Functions

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:59.120
Framework and run it in
these other container-based

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:01.140
environments.

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:04.730
Let me recap what Cloud Run is
and what Cloud Run on GKE is.

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:07.430
So one is it's
serverless containers.

00:27:07.430 --> 00:27:11.600
You build a container using
by-- specifying a Docker file.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:15.290
You can now run that on our
Cloud Run, our server container

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:17.060
infrastructure.

00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:19.010
So you wrote some code.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:20.450
You wrote a Docker file.

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:22.190
You did Docker
Build, or you built

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:23.830
it using some other product.

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:26.210
And now you deploy that
to our infrastructure.

00:27:26.210 --> 00:27:29.110
That will scale
automatically for you.

00:27:29.110 --> 00:27:31.070
It takes HTTP requests.

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:34.690
You only pay for the
resources that you use.

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:36.940
You can bring your own
container, as I just mentioned.

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:38.650
You're going to
write a Docker file.

00:27:38.650 --> 00:27:41.980
With a Docker file, if you're
not familiar with containers,

00:27:41.980 --> 00:27:43.630
it's not that scary,
but it gives you

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:45.430
a huge amount of flexibility.

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:48.580
So you can put in whatever
binaries that you want.

00:27:48.580 --> 00:27:51.220
You can specify specific
rules, and it gives you way

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:53.200
more control than
you can get just

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:54.920
by writing some source code.

00:27:54.920 --> 00:27:58.000
You can say, hey,
apt get install blah,

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:01.300
and that will allow you to
put custom stuff in there.

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:03.960
And then the last piece is
it says Cloud Run and Cloud

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:05.340
Run on GKE.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:07.710
And I'd encourage you to
read up on Cloud Run or Cloud

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:09.900
Run on GKE after the talk.

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:12.150
But you have this
common experience

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:16.180
where you can build a
container and deploy it.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:17.800
You deploy it to
either Cloud Run,

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:19.830
which is a fully
managed environment that

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:21.408
runs on our infrastructure.

00:28:21.408 --> 00:28:22.950
It's actually the
same infrastructure

00:28:22.950 --> 00:28:25.410
that Cloud Functions runs on.

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:27.750
Or you can deploy it
to Cloud Run on GKE,

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:31.620
which runs inside of your Google
Kubernetes Engine cluster.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:33.450
And why would you
want to do that?

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:36.180
Well, it gives you an even
bigger degree of control.

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:38.770
And we'll talk about
that in a second.

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:40.650
So you've got these
two environments

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:43.710
that can run containers.

00:28:43.710 --> 00:28:46.230
How do I run my function
in those environments?

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:48.740
Well, remember that
Cloud Functions--

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:51.920
when we build your function,
it's really just a container,

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:54.620
at the end of the day, that
runs on our serverless container

00:28:54.620 --> 00:28:57.410
infrastructure.

00:28:57.410 --> 00:29:00.740
You can take full control
over that container using

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:03.050
the Functions Framework
by specifying a runtime,

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:05.060
by specifying an
operating system.

00:29:05.060 --> 00:29:07.220
You can build that
container yourself,

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:12.040
and now you can deploy that to
Cloud Run and Cloud Run on GKE.

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:14.870
So whereas with Cloud
Functions, you just

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:17.000
write some source code,
you type a command,

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:20.067
and you deploy it, if you want
to go down this path, which

00:29:20.067 --> 00:29:22.400
I think is a super interesting
path because it gives you

00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:25.970
a way more flexibility, you
can write your function code,

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:28.310
add the Functions Framework,
write a Docker file,

00:29:28.310 --> 00:29:29.350
build it, and deploy it.

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:31.267
And it's actually quite
a seamless experience.

