00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:23.327
And we should have another URL.

00:37:23.327 --> 00:37:25.160
STEWART REICHLING:
Serving traffic at-- yep,

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:27.105
it gives you another URL.

00:37:27.105 --> 00:37:27.980
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Yep.

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:30.320
There we go-- hello
cloud run in US Central.

00:37:30.320 --> 00:37:35.150
And then finally, we
had the same going

00:37:35.150 --> 00:37:39.030
into my Kubernetes,
cluster which is here,

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:41.370
which I've already set up.

00:37:41.370 --> 00:37:45.530
If we come to here and
we paste that in there--

00:37:45.530 --> 00:37:46.530
STEWART REICHLING: Nice.

00:37:46.530 --> 00:37:49.210
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Hello from
Cloud Run on GKE in US West.

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:50.377
STEWART REICHLING: OK, cool.

00:37:50.377 --> 00:37:54.710
So you just took the same
code, you added a framework,

00:37:54.710 --> 00:37:57.230
and you added a Docker
file, and you deployed it

00:37:57.230 --> 00:37:58.950
to three different environments.

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:01.190
So we've established
that you have portability

00:38:01.190 --> 00:38:04.070
among these environments when
you're using the Functions

00:38:04.070 --> 00:38:06.140
Framework and you just
want to write functions.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:07.682
Can we go back to
the slides, please?

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:12.140
So your functions are portable.

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:13.640
You can run them
on Cloud Functions.

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:14.770
You can run them locally.

00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:16.950
You can run them on these
different environments.

00:38:16.950 --> 00:38:19.510
But what's the big deal, right?

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:20.570
Portability-- so what?

00:38:20.570 --> 00:38:21.800
Who cares?

00:38:21.800 --> 00:38:23.282
Well, for one,
these environments

00:38:23.282 --> 00:38:25.240
are all serverless, which
means you have a very

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:27.320
simple developer experience.

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:31.100
You have auto scaling to
meet traffic up and down.

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:34.630
You have HTTP triggers
where you have a URL

00:38:34.630 --> 00:38:36.730
that you can hit to
make your function run.

00:38:36.730 --> 00:38:38.920
Then you also don't have
to manage infrastructure.

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:41.530
And I'd be kind of lying if
I put a little green check

00:38:41.530 --> 00:38:42.710
in the bottom right box.

00:38:42.710 --> 00:38:45.820
You do have to
manage a GKE cluster.

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:48.580
It's still significantly
easier than if you

00:38:48.580 --> 00:38:51.850
tried to do this
yourself, but just

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:53.920
be aware that if you want
to do Cloud Run in GKE,

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:55.330
you have to have a GKE cluster.

00:38:55.330 --> 00:38:57.580
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: But there's
a good reason for using--

00:38:57.580 --> 00:39:00.550
we haven't really touched
on if we're using like GKE.

00:39:00.550 --> 00:39:03.190
If you wanted to
particularly use GPUs--

00:39:03.190 --> 00:39:03.730
like say--

00:39:03.730 --> 00:39:04.840
STEWART REICHLING: I'm
going to get to that.

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:05.400
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Oh, OK.

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:06.190
STEWART REICHLING:
That's coming.

00:39:06.190 --> 00:39:07.895
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Oh,
that's for your show.

00:39:07.895 --> 00:39:08.350
STEWART REICHLING: OK.

00:39:08.350 --> 00:39:11.080
So how do you think about these
three different environments?

00:39:11.080 --> 00:39:13.720
The first one-- Cloud
Functions go from source code

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:16.000
to a running function
within minutes.

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:17.740
What we just showed
you is pretty simple.

00:39:17.740 --> 00:39:19.740
You write a couple lines,
issue a command, boom.

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:21.400
You're good to go.

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:24.040
Cloud Run-- why would you
want to do it on Cloud Run?

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:26.140
Well, when you write
that Docker file,

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:27.820
you can put arbitrary
stuff in there.

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:29.260
It gives you
fine-grained control

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:31.660
over what actually goes
inside of the container.

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:33.580
Maybe you need a
very specific binary.

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:36.500
Maybe you need to have stuff
built in a certain way.

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:38.320
Well, if you write
a Docker file,

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:40.360
build it, deploy
to Cloud Run, you

00:39:40.360 --> 00:39:44.778
have an autoscaling function
that includes whatever custom

00:39:44.778 --> 00:39:46.070
stuff you need to put in there.

00:39:46.070 --> 00:39:48.700
So it gives you more
flexibility, more power,

00:39:48.700 --> 00:39:50.740
a little bit more
responsibility in terms

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:55.720
of what you put into that Docker
file, but also more power.

00:39:55.720 --> 00:39:59.680
Cloud Run on GKE, very
similar sort of story.

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:02.590
You can run it now in
a self-managed cluster.

00:40:02.590 --> 00:40:04.360
Within that cluster,
your function

00:40:04.360 --> 00:40:06.370
will scale up and
scale down according

00:40:06.370 --> 00:40:07.630
to the size of your cluster.

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:10.150
And that cluster can
obviously grow, too,

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:12.670
as your needs change.

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:15.980
But you can run it in that
cluster with even more control.

00:40:15.980 --> 00:40:18.190
For example, you need
specialized instance types

00:40:18.190 --> 00:40:20.020
like GPUs.

00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:22.930
Now you can have this
function code that you wrote,

00:40:22.930 --> 00:40:25.000
deployed it to Cloud
Run on GKE where

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:28.060
you have an instance
pool that includes GPUs

00:40:28.060 --> 00:40:31.030
or more CPUs, specialized
instance types.

00:40:31.030 --> 00:40:33.770
And your function can
use those resources.

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:35.680
And so this is a pretty
cool way of thinking

00:40:35.680 --> 00:40:38.830
about the portability aspect,
is I can write my function code

00:40:38.830 --> 00:40:40.330
once, and I realize,
hey, I actually

00:40:40.330 --> 00:40:41.420
need this specialized thing.

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:41.920
OK.

00:40:41.920 --> 00:40:44.350
Well, I can move it to Cloud
Run, and I can do that.

00:40:44.350 --> 00:40:46.390
Maybe I need something
even more specialized.

00:40:46.390 --> 00:40:48.040
I can move it to
Cloud Run on GKE,

00:40:48.040 --> 00:40:49.870
and I can do that
too without needing

00:40:49.870 --> 00:40:53.150
to change my function code.

00:40:53.150 --> 00:40:54.920
As we start to wrap
up, I just want

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:57.200
to highlight that this
is just a first step.

00:40:57.200 --> 00:41:00.033
What we showed you
here is just Node 10.

00:41:00.033 --> 00:41:01.700
I'm sure a lot of
people in the audience

00:41:01.700 --> 00:41:04.622
also want Python, Go, Java.

00:41:04.622 --> 00:41:06.080
All of the runtimes
that we support

00:41:06.080 --> 00:41:07.872
on Cloud Functions,
the new ones that we're

00:41:07.872 --> 00:41:10.460
going to be releasing, are based
on this Functions Framework

00:41:10.460 --> 00:41:13.280
model that's going to give
you that nice local developer

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:14.090
experience.

00:41:14.090 --> 00:41:16.790
It's going to give you that
portability to different cloud

00:41:16.790 --> 00:41:18.717
environments.

00:41:18.717 --> 00:41:20.300
Hey, one thing I
should call out, too,

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:23.960
is I showed Cloud Run on
GKE, and I mentioned earlier

00:41:23.960 --> 00:41:26.600
that these are
Knative environments.

00:41:26.600 --> 00:41:28.100
There are other
Knative environments

00:41:28.100 --> 00:41:32.250
provided by companies
that are not Google.

00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:35.100
IBM, for example, has
a Knative environment.

00:41:35.100 --> 00:41:39.080
The container that you build
can run on that environment too.

00:41:39.080 --> 00:41:41.690
So this isn't just portability
within Google Cloud.

00:41:41.690 --> 00:41:45.230
It's also portability among
Knative environments provided

00:41:45.230 --> 00:41:46.910
by other providers.

00:41:46.910 --> 00:41:48.410
JUSTIN GRAYSTON:
Or even on premise.

00:41:48.410 --> 00:41:49.500
STEWART REICHLING: Yes.

00:41:49.500 --> 00:41:52.580
You can run Knative in
your cluster on premises,

00:41:52.580 --> 00:41:55.140
and you can run
your function there.

00:41:55.140 --> 00:41:56.598
So back to the runtimes.

00:41:56.598 --> 00:41:58.640
All new runtimes that
we're going to be releasing

00:41:58.640 --> 00:41:59.970
are going to follow this model.

00:41:59.970 --> 00:42:02.190
I'll give you that local
developer experience.

00:42:02.190 --> 00:42:04.640
It'll give you that portability.

00:42:04.640 --> 00:42:07.273
Python, we're planning
to-- when Python 3.8 comes,

00:42:07.273 --> 00:42:08.690
out that's going
to be a Functions

00:42:08.690 --> 00:42:11.080
Framework-based model.

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:13.810
We also see this Functions
Framework as a great way

00:42:13.810 --> 00:42:16.240
that we can start to integrate
additional functionality

00:42:16.240 --> 00:42:18.020
into your functions.

00:42:18.020 --> 00:42:23.013
So one example here is I want
to be able to see my trace data.

00:42:23.013 --> 00:42:25.430
Imagine I have three or four
functions calling each other.

00:42:25.430 --> 00:42:27.097
I want to see the
lifecycle of a request

00:42:27.097 --> 00:42:29.500
as it passes through
those different functions

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:32.260
so that I can see which
one takes the longest.

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:34.270
Where does this
thing get caught up?

00:42:34.270 --> 00:42:37.330
We can add those
things to the module--

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:38.950
that is, the
Functions Framework--

00:42:38.950 --> 00:42:40.720
so that in each language,
you suddenly get

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:43.300
this functionality,
for free, built in.

00:42:43.300 --> 00:42:45.370
And when I say
free, these services

00:42:45.370 --> 00:42:47.290
do sometimes have
a cost, but I mean

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:50.228
that you don't have to actually
add this boilerplate code

00:42:50.228 --> 00:42:51.520
to every one of your functions.

00:42:51.520 --> 00:42:55.030
It just comes nicely integrated.

00:42:55.030 --> 00:42:59.280
And then also, a lot of the
talk today was based on HTTP.

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:03.150
We also support events, so
an event style signature.

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:06.390
And we do have more work to
do on bringing these events

00:43:06.390 --> 00:43:08.410
to other platforms.

00:43:08.410 --> 00:43:11.070
But we're working
with the CNCF, which

00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:14.790
is the Cloud Native Computing
Foundations CloudEvents spec.

00:43:14.790 --> 00:43:16.230
So we support that today.

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:18.590
If you send CloudEvents
to the function,

00:43:18.590 --> 00:43:20.430
the Functions Framework
will automatically

00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:24.127
convert those into objects
relevant to your language.

00:43:24.127 --> 00:43:25.710
And these are the
pieces that we think

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:29.110
are really key to providing
a good functions experience.

00:43:29.110 --> 00:43:29.915
It should be easy.

00:43:29.915 --> 00:43:32.040
It should be well integrated
so that you don't have

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:33.248
to write a bunch boilerplate.

00:43:33.248 --> 00:43:35.090
It should support
events, obviously.

00:43:35.090 --> 00:43:36.690
And we think we
can do all of this

00:43:36.690 --> 00:43:39.490
through the Functions Framework.

00:43:39.490 --> 00:43:41.465
So with that, as
we wrap up, there

00:43:41.465 --> 00:43:42.840
are a couple of
sessions that I'd

00:43:42.840 --> 00:43:44.423
point you to if you
want to learn more

00:43:44.423 --> 00:43:45.720
about what you saw today.

00:43:45.720 --> 00:43:47.320
Most of these have
already happened,

00:43:47.320 --> 00:43:48.862
but they're going
to be up on YouTube

00:43:48.862 --> 00:43:50.370
as well if they're
not up already.

00:43:50.370 --> 00:43:52.950
So what's new in serverless
compute goes much more

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:55.568
into App Engine, Cloud
Functions, Cloud Run,

00:43:55.568 --> 00:43:56.610
what's all the new stuff.

00:43:56.610 --> 00:43:58.470
Some really cool demos
in there, too, that

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:03.000
show us scaling up from zero
to almost 10,000 instances

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:04.860
to handle a request
load within, like,

00:44:04.860 --> 00:44:07.290
five seconds, which is
pretty mind-blowing.

00:44:07.290 --> 00:44:09.000
We also have a Cloud
Run session which

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:11.910
will go much deeper on how you
actually use this product, what

00:44:11.910 --> 00:44:15.000
are the features, and a Cloud
Run on GKE session-- similar,

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:18.870
but about Cloud Run on GKE.

00:44:18.870 --> 00:44:20.880
So with that, I
want to say thanks.

00:44:20.880 --> 00:44:21.690
Some links here.

00:44:21.690 --> 00:44:23.232
This is all going
to be up on YouTube

00:44:23.232 --> 00:44:25.800
if you need to
capture those later.

00:44:25.800 --> 00:44:30.030
Cloud Functions docs, also the
Functions Framework Repository,

00:44:30.030 --> 00:44:32.520
which is live today on
GitHub, and the Cloud Run

00:44:32.520 --> 00:44:37.110
docs, great ways to get
familiar with the products.

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:39.060
Your feedback
greatly appreciated.

